(Stage 1c — a predicate result appended to a list is tagged TAG_BOOLEAN,
 not TAG_INTEGER, so a later `is a boolean` recognises it. Each element of
 `flags` is itself a type predicate result; if any were mis-tagged as a
 number, `f is a boolean` would fold false and print "N". Exercises the
 TypeCheck arms of prescan_expr_tag / emit_time_expr_tag and the append
 element-type classifier, reached via
 `append <value> is a <noun> to <list>`, plus the negation path
 `is not a <noun>` via UnaryOp(Not, TypeCheck).)

a list called m is [1, "two", 3].
a list called flags is [].
For each item in m,
  append item is a number to flags,
  append item is not a text to flags.
For each f in flags,
  if f is a boolean, print "B",
  otherwise print "N".